          "text": "I added `eval` to the forbidden list in `checks.json`, so any new direct `eval(...)` call in `src` will now fail the check you run with `python3 check.py`.\n\nI didn't need to change `check.py`. It already reads the code's structure instead of searching the text, and it only flags direct calls to a bare `eval`. So comments, strings and `obj.eval(...)` method calls still pass.\n\nI tested it on a temporary copy of the repo:\n- **Should pass:** a file with `eval` in a comment, in a string, and as `obj.eval(...)` passed (exit code 0).\n- **Should fail:** a file containing `eval(\"1+1\")` failed and was reported by name (exit code 1).\n- **The real repo:** still passes, since there are no `eval` calls in `src`.\n\nBecause it only checks code structure, it won't catch indirect uses such as `builtins.eval(...)` or `f = eval; f(...)`. That matches how the check already treats any forbidden built-in."
